  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Time Scaling of Exponentials",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert time-scaled exponentials by rewriting e^{$s(at)$} as $e^{(as)t}$ and interpreting a as changing the growth, decay, or oscillation rate in continuous time.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time Scaling of Exponentials",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time Scaling of Exponentials",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When the exponent contains a time-scaled input, rewrite the expression so the scale factor multiplies the exponent coefficient. The core identity is $e^{s(at)} = e^{(as)t}$, which keeps the exponential family the same while changing its rate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{s(at)} = e^{(as)t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the real exponential $e^{-t}$. If you replace $t$ by $2t$, the result is $e^{-2t}$, so the same decay shape happens twice as fast and is compressed toward $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-t} \\rightarrow e^{-2t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That same rate change rule gives the general interpretation: if $a>1$, the exponential evolves faster, and if $0<a<1$, it evolves more slowly. The multiplier changes how quickly the curve moves in time, not the exponential family itself.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same idea also applies when the exponent is imaginary. For a complex exponential, $e^{j\\omega(at)} = e^{j(a\\omega)t}$, so the factor $a$ changes the oscillation rate in exactly the same way that it changes the growth or decay rate in a real exponential.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega(at)} = e^{j(a\\omega)t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time Scaling of Exponentials",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Rewriting a Time-Scaled Real Exponential",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $x(t)=e^{-3(2t)}$ in standard form and state how its decay compares with $e^{-3t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the factor $2$ inside the exponent. For a time-scaled real exponential, rewrite the signal by multiplying the exponent coefficient by the scale factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-3(2t)}=e^{(-3\\cdot 2)t}=e^{-6t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compared with $e^{-3t}$, the exponent coefficient changes from $-3$ to $-6$. The rewritten signal is still the same exponential family, but its decay happens twice as fast because the coefficient is larger in magnitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the time-scaled signal is compressed toward $t=0$ and has unchanged amplitude. The only change is the decay rate, which is faster in the rewritten form $e^{-6t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time Scaling of Exponentials",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Faster and Slower Exponential Decay",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compare $x_1(t)=e^{-2(3t)}$ and $x_2(t)=e^{-2(0.5t)}$ with the unscaled signal $e^{-2t}$; rewrite each and decide which decays fastest, which is unchanged, and which decays slowest.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the scale factor inside the exponent. Rewrite each signal by multiplying the base decay coefficient by that factor, then compare the resulting coefficients.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}x_1(t)&=e^{-2(3t)}=e^{-6t}\\\\e^{-2t}&=e^{-2t}\\\\x_2(t)&=e^{-2(0.5t)}=e^{-t}\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The effective decay coefficients are $-6$, $-2$, and $-1$. Since the more negative coefficient decays faster, $x_1(t)$ decays fastest, $e^{-2t}$ is unchanged, and $x_2(t)$ decays slowest.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That ordering matches the scaling rule: $a>1$ compresses the waveform toward $t=0$, while $0<a<1$ stretches it away from $t=0$. The same coefficient-multiplication rule explains both the rewrite and the decay-speed comparison.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time Scaling of Exponentials",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Scaling the Oscillation Rate of a Complex Exponential",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $x(t)=e^{j4(2t)}$ in standard form and state how the oscillation rate compares with $e^{j4t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the factor $2$ inside the complex exponential. Use the same coefficient-multiplication rule as before: multiply the frequency coefficient by the time-scaling factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j4(2t)}=e^{j(4\\cdot 2)t}=e^{j8t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compared with $e^{j4t}$, the angular frequency changes from $4$ to $8$. The rewritten signal is still the same complex exponential family, but it oscillates twice as fast because the coefficient was multiplied by $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the time-scaled complex exponential keeps the same amplitude while its oscillation rate increases. The general rule is still $e^{s(at)}=e^{(as)t}$; here the imaginary part of $s$ makes that rate change show up as faster oscillation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time Scaling of Exponentials",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Scaling Both the Envelope and Oscillation of a Damped Sinusoid",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $x(t)=e^{(-1+j4)(2t)}$ in standard form and identify how both the envelope and oscillation compare with $e^{(-1+j4)t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the scale factor $2$ multiplying $t$. Apply the same rule to every part of the complex exponent: multiply both the real part that controls decay and the imaginary part that controls oscillation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{(-1+j4)(2t)}=e^{(-2+j8)t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The real part changes from $-1$ to $-2$, so the envelope is $e^{-2t}$ instead of $e^{-t}$ and decays twice as fast.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The imaginary part changes from $j4$ to $j8$, so the oscillation rate doubles. The initial amplitude is still $1$; time scaling changes the rate, not the starting height.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the standard form is $e^{(-2+j8)t}$, and the combined interpretation is faster decay together with faster oscillation, exactly as the rule $e^{s(at)}=e^{(as)t}$ predicts.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
